posts.body:
Hello. I'm trying to make a custom item menu with "Show Choices" command. I want to make it somehow to remember exactly what item was used just so player wouldn't have to scroll again if he needs to use that item again, as well as to make it possible to use an item without opening a menu again (most of my items are just common events).
After some forum browsing i found nothing, so i tried to implement it via script.
Code:
$gameMessage.setChoices(['A', 'B', 'C'], $gameVariables.value(101), -2);
$gameMessage.setChoiceBackground(2);
$gameMessage.setChoicePositionType(0);
$gameMessage.setChoiceCallback(n => {
  $gameVariables.setValue(101, n);
});
this.setWaitMode('message');
It works, but creates another problem. I'm no good with javascript. I have no idea how to script the choices themself. I assume i will have to just make a branching choice with the '101' variable, but there should be some easier way to do it probably? Also, if using this script, how can i hide certain choices? Like, can i hide a "B" option if some switch is turned off? It's a menu script after all, so this is key thing for it. Thank you for reading this!

Okay, I made two completely different mini-plugins for you to choose from. (You could even use both, if you want.)
This one replaces the menu scene with the item scene. You press escape or click the menu button, you get the item scene and only the item scene without a category selector.
JavaScript:
Scene_Map.prototype.callMenu = function() {
  SceneManager.push(Scene_Item);
};
(function(old) {
  Scene_Item.prototype.create = function() {
    old.call(this);
    this._categoryWindow.deactivate();
    this._categoryWindow.hide();
    this.activateItemWindow();
    this._itemWindow.select(0);
    this._itemWindow.setHandler("cancel",this.popScene.bind(this));
  }
})(Scene_Item.prototype.create);
And it turned out a bit longer than I expected, but this makes the game remember your last choice in each Show Choices:
JavaScript:
(function(old) {
  Game_Interpreter.prototype.setupChoices = function(params) {
    let key = [];
    if(this._depth > 0 || !this.character()) {
      key = ['common', this._commonEventId, this._index];
    } else {
      key = [$gameMap.mapId(), this.character().eventId(), this.character()._pageIndex, this._index];
    };
    $gameTemp.savedChoices = $gameTemp.savedChoices || {};
    if(key in $gameTemp.savedChoices) {
      params = params.clone();
      params[2] = $gameTemp.savedChoices[key];
    }
    old.call(this,params);
    let callback = $gameMessage._choiceCallback;
    $gameMessage.setChoiceCallback(n => (callback(n), $gameTemp.savedChoices[key] = n))
  };
})(Game_Interpreter.prototype.setupChoices);
(function(old) {
  Game_Interpreter.prototype.command117 = function(params) {
    old.call(this,params);
    if($dataCommonEvents[params[0]] && this._childInterpreter) {
      this._childInterpreter._commonEventId = params[0];
    }
    return true;
  };
})(Game_Interpreter.prototype.command117);
(function(old) {
  Game_Interpreter.prototype.setupReservedCommonEvent = function() {
    if($gameTemp.isCommonEventReserved()) {
      this._commonEventId = $gameTemp._commonEventQueue[0];
    }
    old.call(this);
  };
})(Game_Interpreter.prototype.setupReservedCommonEvent);
With that,
every
Show Choices in your game will have a separate memory for the last choice the player selected. I didn't check for the case of troop events – if it does something absolutely insane when you put a choice in a troop event, don't be surprised.
I did basic testing on both of them in MZ and they seem to work. I can't be certain that there are unexpected side-effects that I missed. Let me know if you run into any issues.
